In-Reply-To: <vhf_qY4tvX0.rpt-LA-2BwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <vhf_qY4tvX0.VSinxcoGAQAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com> <vhf_qY4tvX0.rpt-LA-2BwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/vhf_qY4tvX0/m/cVXvIxbrBwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Depends on the specific model you want, of course, but I would usually also use the parent levels.
E.g. school, class, student: the class wide performance is something you want to explicitly model (even when modelling student level performance) because it can depends on the teacher or the classroom facilities. Similarly, the school level can be a proxy for resources or general student background.

Haakon

